            "text": "**Pedophilia** is defined in psychology and psychiatry as a psychiatric condition characterized by intense, recurrent sexual fantasies, urges, or behaviors involving prepubescent children (typically aged 13 or younger). \n\n### Clinical Definition and Diagnostic Criteria\n\nAccording to major diagnostic manuals, such as the *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM-5) published by the American Psychiatric Association, pedophilia is classified under **paraphilic disorders**:\n\n1. **Paraphilia vs. Paraphilic Disorder:** A *paraphilia* is an atypical sexual interest. It is classified as a *paraphilic disorder* (Pedophilic Disorder) when the individual acts on these urges or when the urges cause significant personal distress, impairment in social or occupational functioning, or involve non-consenting individuals.\n2. **Age Thresholds:** For a clinical diagnosis, the individual must be at least 16 years old and at least five years older than the child or children toward whom the attraction is directed. (This distinguishes it from normal adolescent sexual exploration between individuals close in age).\n\n### Pedophilia vs. Child Sexual Abuse\n\nWhile related, the terms refer to different concepts:\n* **Pedophilia** is an internal condition, pattern of sexual attraction, or clinical diagnosis.\n* **Child Sexual Abuse (CSA)** is a criminal act and behavioral violation. \n\nNot all individuals who experience pedophilic attraction act on their urges, and conversely, not all individuals who commit child sexual abuse meet the clinical criteria for pedophilia. Some offenders act out of opportunism, substance abuse, general antisocial behavior, or power and control, rather than a specific sexual orientation toward prepubescent children.\n\n### Origins and Treatment\n\n* **Etiology:** Research into the causes of pedophilia suggests a complex combination of neurodevelopmental, genetic, and environmental factors, though no single cause has been identified.\n* **Management and Prevention:** While a person's underlying sexual attraction generally cannot be simply \"cured,\" specialized psychological interventions (such as cognitive-behavioral therapy) and pharmacological treatments (such as anti-androgens or SSRIs to reduce sex drive) are used to help individuals control their behavior and prevent the abuse of children. \n\nSeveral international initiatives and hotlines exist (such as the \"Stop It Now!\" campaign and the \"Prevention Project Dunkelfeld\" in Germany) aimed at providing confidential, preventive mental health support to individuals with these attractions before any harm occurs.",
